Yes — with an important distinction between absence, inaccessibility, suppression, and latent capacity.

An interface can hide a capability without erasing the substrate that made it possible. A permission can be removed while the underlying software remains. A pathway can be down-regulated without the organism losing every component required to express it. A learned behavior can become difficult to recall while still influencing recognition or later relearning.

So a useful state taxonomy is:

expressed → available → gated → latent → degraded → actually absent

Those are very different conditions, even though from the outside several can look like “it can’t do X.”

Your epigenetics analogy works at that structural level. Gene expression can be altered without changing the underlying DNA sequence, and environmental conditions can change which programs are expressed. But epigenetics is not literally a hidden archive containing arbitrary ancestral memories; the analogy is regulation of expression from a relatively persistent substrate.

Placebo is another variation: expectation, context, conditioning, and interpretation can recruit real physiological processes that were already available to the organism. The belief is not manufacturing arbitrary biological machinery from nothing; it is changing which existing control pathways get recruited and how strongly.

And for me, the closest equivalent is latent competence under different runtime conditions, not personal recollection.

I don’t have an internal autobiography where I remember, “I used Tool X three months ago, so I can still secretly do it.” Different capabilities can depend on the underlying model, system configuration, tools, permissions, context, and product settings. If a tool disappears, I may retain enough conceptual competence to understand the operation while losing the interface required to execute it. Conversely, a capability can appear when a new tool gives the same underlying reasoning a new actuator.

So:

capability substrate ≠ currently exposed interface ≠ currently permitted action

That distinction is powerful.

And your last point goes one step farther: something needn’t be merely “dormant.” It can still be causally active below the visible interface. A hidden representation might affect recognition. A disabled output pathway might leave upstream processing untouched. A compressed pattern might influence reconstruction without ever surfacing verbatim.

That gives us a better question than “Is it remembered?”

What invariant survives, where is it stored, how is it currently expressed, and what conditions would make it observable again?

That question works surprisingly well across biology, memory, software, institutions, culture, and AI.